Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedreciotest.shoddy — test program for seedrecio.shoddy
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the cuttletest / reckonertest / seedtest pattern:
Rem it prints what it is doing and stops the run at the first assertion
Rem that fails, ending with ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when it does not.
Rem
Rem WHAT IS ACTUALLY UNDER TEST. seedrecio is the first seed that opens
Rem something and keeps it open across lines, so the suite has two
Rem halves that other seed suites do not need:
Rem
Rem   * THE RESOURCE HALF — that a file opened at the prompt survives
Rem     UNDO, that CLOSE and BOUND (reckoner's own words, not this
Rem     seed's) reach it, and that no handle number is ever on the
Rem     stack. reckonertest asserts this over a hand-bound resource;
Rem     here it is asserted over one a SESSION opened by typing.
Rem   * THE LAYOUT HALF — that a schema typed at a prompt drives the
Rem     generic reader and writer correctly, and that every way of
Rem     mistyping one is a refusal naming the field.
Rem
Rem THE GUARD LIST IS THE TEST LIST, as everywhere else: every refusal
Rem seedrecio.shoddy invents is walked exactly once, in the two-part
Rem shape the seed suites established — the line refuses for the stated
Rem reason with the state as it was, AND a further line evaluates
Rem afterwards, because "the mill did not die" is the actual contract.
Rem
Rem WHERE THAT SHAPE DOES NOT APPLY, and it is worth naming: Refuses
Rem runs the same line more than once, which is free for a word that
Rem answers and not free for one that OPENS or CLOSES something. Those
Rem are asserted by running the line once and reading the state after.
Rem
Rem Fixtures: none committed. The suite writes and deletes its own,
Rem beside the other suites' scratch files in tst/csv/.
Rem
Rem Run: bin\mill run tst\seedreciotest.shoddy      (from the repo root)
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/cuttle.shoddy"
Include "../machines/reckoner.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seq.shoddy"
Include "../machines/str.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedrecio.shoddy"

Rem ---- reading an answer ------------------------------------------------
Rem The same shapes seedtest.shoddy reads with, repeated here rather than
Rem shared, in the house pattern of each test file standing alone.

Def Ran(st As RckState, line As String) As RckState
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            st2
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            st2
        Case Else
            st

Def Runs(st As RckState, lines As List Of String) As RckState
    Fold(lines, st, Ran)

Def Bad(st As RckState, line As String) As Boolean
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def Why(st As RckState, line As String) As String
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            why
        Case Else
            ""

Def Text(st As RckState) As String
    Join(Map(CutCells(RckStack(st)), Fn(c) => CutShow(c, RckFmt(st))), " | ")

Def Q(s As String) As String
    Chr(34) & s & Chr(34)

Rem The two-part abort-safety assertion. Safe only for lines that do not
Rem open or close anything, because it evaluates the line three times.
Def Refuses(st As RckState, line As String, part As String) As Boolean
    Bad(st, line) And Instr(Why(st, line), part) > 0 And Text(Ran(st, line)) = Text(st) And Text(Ran(Ran(st, line), "1 1 +")) = Text(Ran(st, "1 1 +"))

Rem ---- the fixture ------------------------------------------------------

Def TmpPath() As String
    "tst/csv/_seedreciotest.tmp"

Def OtherPath() As String
    "tst/csv/_seedreciotest_other.tmp"

Def JunkPath() As String
    "tst/csv/_seedreciotest_junk.tmp"

Def Wipe()
    If FileExists(TmpPath()) Then
        DeleteFile(TmpPath())
    If FileExists(OtherPath()) Then
        DeleteFile(OtherPath())
    If FileExists(JunkPath()) Then
        DeleteFile(JunkPath())

Rem One of each kind, so the record exercises all three transfers and
Rem the sizes add up to something worth checking: 8 + 12 + 1 = 21.
Def Schema() As String
    "{ { " & Q("id") & " " & Q("number") & " } { " & Q("who") & " " & Q("str") & " 12 } { " & Q("paid") & " " & Q("bool") & " } }"

Def RecSize() As Number
    21

Def Opened(st As RckState, path As String, nm As String) As RckState
    Ran(st, Q(path) & " " & Schema() & " " & Q(nm) & " RECOPEN")

Def ByteLen(path As String) As Number
    Len(ReadFile(path))

Def Main()
    Wipe()
    Let s0 = RckSeedRecio(RckNew())

    Print("--- the seed is in the dictionary, grouped and documented ---")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "RECOPEN") And RckKnows(s0, "RECGET") And RckKnows(s0, "RECAPPEND"), "THE WORDS ARE THERE")
    Assert(Contains(RckWords(s0), "-- seed-recio --"), "AND THEY HAVE A GROUP OF THEIR OWN")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => WordEffect(e) <> "" And WordDesc(e) <> ""), "NO WORD IS WITHOUT AN EFFECT LINE AND A DESCRIPTION")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => Length(RckHelp(s0, WordName(e))) = 3), "AND HELP ANSWERS FOR EVERY ONE OF THEM")
    Rem The effect strings say "name", never "handle": what a session
    Rem holds is a string it chose, and the vocabulary has to say so.
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => Instr(Lower(WordEffect(e)), "handle") = 0), "NO WORD'S EFFECT STRING MENTIONS A HANDLE")
    Rem There is no RECCLOSE. One way to shut a resource is the whole
    Rem reason reckoner's table is uniform, and a second spelling here
    Rem would be a seed disagreeing with the core about who owns closing.
    Assert(Not RckKnows(s0, "RECCLOSE"), "AND THERE IS NO RECCLOSE — CLOSE IS THE CORE'S WORD AND THE ONLY ONE")

    Print("--- opening makes the file, and binds it under the name given ---")
    Let o1 = Opened(s0, TmpPath(), "db")
    Assert(RckBoundTo(o1, "db"), "RECOPEN ANSWERS, AND BINDS UNDER THE NAME IT WAS GIVEN")
    Assert(FileExists(TmpPath()), "AND MADE THE FILE, SINCE A NEW LEDGER IS AN ORDINARY THING TO WANT")
    Rem The same PATH twice, under two different names, is refused by the
    Rem runtime rather than by this seed — an open file is not shared for
    Rem writing — and the point is that it arrives as a refusal naming
    Rem the path instead of as a dead mill. Asserted from o1, where the
    Rem first open is genuinely still held.
    Assert(Refuses(o1, Q(TmpPath()) & " " & Schema() & " " & Q("second") & " RECOPEN", "CANNOT OPEN"), "AND THE SAME FILE OPENED AGAIN UNDER A SECOND NAME REFUSES")
    Assert(Not RckBoundTo(Ran(o1, Q(TmpPath()) & " " & Schema() & " " & Q("second") & " RECOPEN"), "second"), "AND BINDS NOTHING WHEN IT DOES")
    Assert(Text(Ran(o1, "BOUND")) = "{ ( " & Q("db") & " " & Q("recio") & " ) }", "AND BOUND SHOWS IT AS A NAME/KIND PAIR")
    Assert(Text(Ran(o1, Q("db") & " RECCOUNT")) = "0", "A NEW FILE HOLDS NO RECORDS")
    Assert(Text(Ran(o1, Q("db") & " RECFIELDS")) = "{ " & Q("id") & " " & Q("who") & " " & Q("paid") & " }", "AND RECFIELDS ANSWERS THE SCHEMA'S NAMES IN ORDER")
    Assert(Text(o1) = "", "AND RECOPEN LEFT NOTHING ON THE STACK — NO HANDLE, NOTHING")

    Print("--- writing and reading a record, all three kinds ---")
    Let a1 = Ran(o1, Q("db") & " { 1 " & Q("GRACE") & " True } RECAPPEND")
    Assert(Text(a1) = "1", "RECAPPEND ANSWERS THE RECORD NUMBER IT BECAME")
    Let a2 = Ran(Ran(a1, "DROP"), Q("db") & " { 2 " & Q("ADA") & " False } RECAPPEND DROP")
    Rem RECCOUNT is BSize over the record size, so this is a real
    Rem measurement of the file and not a tally the seed keeps. The
    Rem on-disk byte length is checked at the end, once the file is shut
    Rem and something other than the seed can open it.
    Assert(Text(Ran(a2, Q("db") & " RECCOUNT")) = "2", "AND THE COUNT FOLLOWS THE FILE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(a2, Q("db") & " 1 RECGET")) = "{ 1 " & Q("GRACE") & " True }", "RECGET READS THE FIRST ROW BACK EXACTLY")
    Assert(Text(Ran(a2, Q("db") & " 2 RECGET")) = "{ 2 " & Q("ADA") & " False }", "AND THE SECOND, INCLUDING A FALSE THAT IS NOT AN EMPTY FIELD")
    Assert(Text(Ran(a2, Q("db") & " RECALL")) = "{ { 1 " & Q("GRACE") & " True } { 2 " & Q("ADA") & " False } }", "AND RECALL READS THE WHOLE FILE IN FILE ORDER")
    Rem The two assertions above are also the padding test: "GRACE" is
    Rem five bytes in a twelve-byte field, so it goes out zero-padded and
    Rem has to come back stripped. Were it not, those comparisons would
    Rem be against a string with seven NULs on the end. There is no
    Rem separate NTH assertion because NTH is seedbuiltin's word and this
    Rem suite seeds only its own.

    Print("--- RECPUT replaces in place, and may extend by exactly one ---")
    Let p1 = Ran(a2, Q("db") & " 1 { 9 " & Q("HOPPER") & " False } RECPUT")
    Assert(Text(Ran(p1, Q("db") & " 1 RECGET")) = "{ 9 " & Q("HOPPER") & " False }", "RECPUT OVERWRITES THE RECORD IT NAMES")
    Assert(Text(Ran(p1, Q("db") & " RECCOUNT")) = "2", "AND DOES NOT CHANGE HOW MANY THERE ARE")
    Let p2 = Ran(p1, Q("db") & " 3 { 3 " & Q("TURING") & " True } RECPUT")
    Assert(Text(Ran(p2, Q("db") & " RECCOUNT")) = "3", "WRITING ONE PAST THE END ADDS A RECORD")
    Rem Seek can go anywhere, so a write past that would leave a hole of
    Rem undefined bytes reading back as garbage — a wrong answer, which
    Rem is worse than a refusal.
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " 9 { 1 " & Q("X") & " True } RECPUT", "WOULD LEAVE A HOLE"), "AND ANYTHING FURTHER IS REFUSED, NAMING THE FURTHEST WRITE ALLOWED")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " 0 { 1 " & Q("X") & " True } RECPUT", "WOULD LEAVE A HOLE"), "AS IS RECORD ZERO, SINCE RECORDS NUMBER FROM ONE")

    Print("--- every read is pre-flighted, because a Get past the end aborts ---")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " 4 RECGET", "THERE IS NO RECORD 4 — THE FILE HOLDS 3"), "A RECORD PAST THE END, NAMING BOTH NUMBERS")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " 0 RECGET", "THERE IS NO RECORD 0"), "AND RECORD ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " -1 RECGET", "RECGET"), "AND A NEGATIVE ONE, WHICH RCKWHOLEARG REFUSES FIRST")

    Print("--- a row is checked against the schema BEFORE any of it is written ---")
    Rem A record half-written is a record corrupted, and PutStr aborts on
    Rem an over-long string, so the whole row is validated first.
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " { 1 " & Q("A") & " } RECAPPEND", "HAS 2 VALUES AND THE SCHEMA HAS 3"), "TOO FEW VALUES, NAMING BOTH COUNTS")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " { 1 " & Q("A") & " True 4 } RECAPPEND", "HAS 4 VALUES AND THE SCHEMA HAS 3"), "AND TOO MANY")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " { " & Q("no") & " " & Q("A") & " True } RECAPPEND", "FIELD 1 (id) WANTS A NUMBER"), "A STRING WHERE A NUMBER GOES, NAMING THE FIELD")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " { 1 5 True } RECAPPEND", "FIELD 2 (who) WANTS A STRING"), "A NUMBER WHERE A STRING GOES")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " { 1 " & Q("A") & " 7 } RECAPPEND", "FIELD 3 (paid) WANTS TRUE OR FALSE"), "AND A NUMBER WHERE A BOOLEAN GOES")
    Rem THE ONE THAT WOULD OTHERWISE END THE SESSION: PutStr aborts
    Rem rather than truncating, so an over-long string has to be caught
    Rem here or not at all.
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("db") & " { 1 " & Q("A NAME MUCH TOO LONG FOR IT") & " True } RECAPPEND", "HOLDS 12 BYTES AND THAT STRING IS 27"), "A STRING LONGER THAN ITS FIELD, NAMING BOTH LENGTHS")
    Assert(Text(Ran(p2, Q("db") & " RECCOUNT")) = "3", "AND NONE OF THOSE REFUSALS WROTE ANYTHING")

    Print("--- a schema is read once, at RECOPEN, and every way of mistyping it refuses ---")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q(OtherPath()) & " { } " & Q("x") & " RECOPEN", "A SCHEMA MUST HAVE AT LEAST ONE FIELD"), "AN EMPTY SCHEMA")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q(OtherPath()) & " { { " & Q("a") & " } } " & Q("x") & " RECOPEN", "FIELD 1 MUST BE"), "A SPEC WITH ONLY A NAME")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q(OtherPath()) & " { { " & Q("a") & " " & Q("money") & " } } " & Q("x") & " RECOPEN", "HAS KIND \"money\""), "AN UNKNOWN KIND, QUOTED BACK")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q(OtherPath()) & " { { " & Q("a") & " " & Q("str") & " } } " & Q("x") & " RECOPEN", "IS A STRING AND NEEDS A WIDTH"), "A STRING FIELD WITH NO WIDTH")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q(OtherPath()) & " { { " & Q("a") & " " & Q("number") & " 8 } } " & Q("x") & " RECOPEN", "TAKES NO WIDTH"), "AND A NUMBER FIELD GIVEN ONE, WHICH IS A MISUNDERSTANDING AND NOT AN EXTRA")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q(OtherPath()) & " { { " & Q("a") & " " & Q("str") & " 0 } } " & Q("x") & " RECOPEN", "NEEDS A WIDTH OF 1 BYTE OR MORE"), "AND A ZERO-WIDTH STRING, WHICH WOULD MAKE EVERY WRITE FATAL")
    Assert(Not FileExists(OtherPath()), "AND NOT ONE OF THOSE REFUSALS MADE A FILE")

    Print("--- a name is a name: taken, unknown, or another machine's ---")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q(OtherPath()) & " " & Schema() & " " & Q("db") & " RECOPEN", "THERE IS ALREADY SOMETHING OPEN CALLED db"), "OPENING OVER A NAME ALREADY BOUND")
    Assert(Not FileExists(OtherPath()), "AND THAT REFUSAL DID NOT OPEN THE SECOND FILE EITHER")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("nope") & " RECCOUNT", "THERE IS NOTHING OPEN CALLED nope"), "A NAME THAT WAS NEVER BOUND")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q("nope") & " RECALL", "THERE IS NOTHING OPEN CALLED nope"), "AND THE SAME FROM ANOTHER WORD")
    Rem A name some other machine bound is not this seed's to read, and
    Rem the kind is what tells them apart.
    Let mixed = RckBind(p2, "pen", "plotter", 0, CutNum(0), Fn() => True)
    Assert(Refuses(mixed, Q("pen") & " RECCOUNT", "pen IS A PLOTTER, NOT A RECORD FILE"), "AND A NAME BOUND BY ANOTHER KIND ENTIRELY")

    Print("--- a file whose length is not a whole number of records is refused at OPEN ---")
    Rem It is a fact about the file and the schema together, so the
    Rem moment they first meet is the earliest honest place to say it.
    WriteFile(JunkPath(), "not a record file")
    Assert(Refuses(p2, Q(JunkPath()) & " " & Schema() & " " & Q("j") & " RECOPEN", "IS NOT A WHOLE NUMBER OF 21-BYTE RECORDS"), "A RAGGED FILE, NAMING ITS LENGTH AND THE RECORD SIZE")
    Assert(Not RckBoundTo(Ran(p2, Q(JunkPath()) & " " & Schema() & " " & Q("j") & " RECOPEN"), "j"), "AND NOTHING WAS LEFT BOUND BY IT")

    Print("--- THE POINT OF ALL OF IT: UNDO cannot strand what a session opened ---")
    Rem reckonertest asserts this over a hand-bound resource. Here the
    Rem session opened it by typing, wrote to it, and undoes the lot.
    Let u1 = Runs(p2, { "1", "2", "3" })
    Assert(Text(u1) = "3 | 2 | 1", "WORK PILES UP ON THE STACK AS USUAL")
    Let u2 = Runs(u1, { "UNDO", "UNDO", "UNDO" })
    Assert(RckBoundTo(u2, "db"), "AND AFTER UNDOING ALL OF IT THE FILE IS STILL OPEN")
    Rem UNDO restores a STACK. It does not un-write a file, and the
    Rem count coming back as 3 rather than 0 is that said plainly.
    Assert(Text(Ran(u2, Q("db") & " RECCOUNT")) = "3", "AND STILL READABLE BY NAME, WITH EVERY RECORD IT WROTE STILL IN IT")

    Print("--- CLOSE is the core's word, and it reaches this seed's resource ---")
    Rem Run once, not through Refuses: closing really closes, and a
    Rem second BClose on a returned handle aborts.
    Let c1 = Ran(u2, Q("db") & " CLOSE")
    Assert(Not RckBoundTo(c1, "db"), "CLOSE LETS THE NAME GO")
    Assert(Text(Ran(c1, "BOUND")) = "{ }", "AND BOUND IS EMPTY AGAIN")
    Rem NOW the file can be measured from outside, which is the check
    Rem RECCOUNT cannot make of itself: three records of 8 + 12 + 1
    Rem bytes, so the schema arithmetic and the file agree.
    Assert(ByteLen(TmpPath()) = 3 * RecSize(), "AND THE SHUT FILE IS THREE RECORDS OF 8 + 12 + 1 BYTES ON DISK")
    Assert(Refuses(c1, Q("db") & " RECCOUNT", "THERE IS NOTHING OPEN CALLED db"), "AND THE WORDS SAY SO AFTERWARDS RATHER THAN READING A SHUT FILE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(c1, "6 7 *")) = "42", "AND THE SESSION GOES ON EVALUATING")
    Rem Re-opening the same path is an ordinary thing to do, and the
    Rem records are still there because the file outlived the binding.
    Let c2 = Opened(c1, TmpPath(), "again")
    Assert(Text(Ran(c2, Q("again") & " RECCOUNT")) = "3", "AND RE-OPENING THE SAME FILE FINDS ITS RECORDS WHERE THEY WERE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(c2, "CLOSEALL")) = "1", "CLOSEALL SHUTS WHAT IS LEFT AND SAYS HOW MANY")

    Wipe()
    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")
